### The program you are writing

{{#if (eq language.id "cpp")}}
Your response is compiled verbatim, as a whole translation unit, and must define
`main`: `int main() { … }` is where your work goes, and every declaration C++ has
is legal where you put it. The C++ standard library is declared in front of your
first line, so `std::vector` and `std::format` need no `#include`.

Every call throws on failure. A failure you expect is a `gg::core::tool_error`,
which is a `std::runtime_error` whose `code()` is an `enum class`; let the rest
escape, and gg names the call that failed.

Required arguments are positional. A call with exactly one optional argument
gives that parameter a default; a call with two or more takes an aggregate you
fill in with designated initializers, naming only the parts you mean. Each module
below is a `namespace` inside `namespace gg`, reached by writing that module's
own `#include` line and then its path in full.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "csharp")}}
Your response is compiled verbatim, as a whole compilation unit: the `using`
lines you need, then top-level statements, which run in the order you wrote them
with every declaration C# has legal beside them.

Every method throws on failure. A failure you expect is a `ToolException` whose
`Code` is an `enum`; let the rest escape, and gg names the call that failed.

Everything gg offers lives in the namespace `Gg`. Write a name in full, or write
its `using` line first and then the last segment of its path. Required arguments
are positional; optional ones have default values and are passed by name; each
module below is a `static class`, so nothing is constructed first.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "java")}}
Your response is a sequence of statements, which gg puts into the body of a
method it declares for you: there is no class to write, no `main` to declare and
no `package` line. Write the `import` lines you would normally write. A helper
type you declare is a local declaration, so write `record Point(int x, int y) {}`
with no access modifier on it.

A failure is an unchecked exception: let one escape, or catch it with
`catch (ToolError failure)` and read `failure.code()`, which is an enum.

Required arguments are positional, and an optional one is an overload: an entry
that shows you several signatures is showing you the ones you may call. A call
with many optional fields takes a builder, and one that takes a list takes a
varargs. Each module below is a class of `static` methods, imported for you
already, so the last segment of a module's path is what you write, and a type one
hands back is nested in it.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "javascript")}}
Your response is evaluated as the body of a function whose parameters include
`gg`, `ToolError` and `lib`. Write statements at the top level; a `return` there
ends your program and its value goes nowhere.

A failed call throws. `ToolError` is the one gg name bound as a value, so
`catch (error) { if (error instanceof ToolError) … }` is the shape that reads its
`tool` and `code`; everything else you discriminate on is an ordinary property.

Required arguments are positional, and a call's optional arguments are the fields
of one options object in the last position. Every module below is bound under
`gg`, so a module's path is the call you write as it stands. The type names a
signature carries are documentation: they do not exist while your program runs.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "kotlin")}}
Your response is compiled as a Kotlin script, in the root package: statements and
declarations side by side at the top level, in whatever order you write them.
There is no class to write and no `main` to declare, so put your work at the top
level. Write the `import` lines you would normally write.

A failure is a `gg.core.ToolError`, and Kotlin has no checked exceptions: let one
escape, catch it with `catch (failure: gg.core.ToolError)`, or wrap a call in
`runCatching`. Its `code` is an enum.

Required arguments are positional, and an optional one is a default argument you
may pass by name; a value that may be absent has a nullable type. Every gg name
is written in full: a module's path below resolves in the root package as it
stands, and an `import` of the one name you want is how you shorten it.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "purescript")}}
Your response is a module whose `main` has type `Effect Unit`, written as a `do`
block. The module name is yours to pick. Import each module you use under its own
full name, as in `import Gg.Files as Gg.Files`, so a module's path below is the
expression you write.

A failed call is thrown rather than returned, so the happy path is already
unwrapped. `Gg.Core.attempt` takes a call and hands back
`Either Gg.Core.ToolError a` for a failure you expect, re-throwing anything that
is not a gg failure; let the rest escape, and gg names the call that failed.

Functions are curried and required arguments are positional; optional ones are
the fields of a record argument, where a field a signature marks `limit?` may be
left out and one written without the `?` must be there. Pass `{}` when you want
none of them — the record is part of the signature.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "python")}}
Your response is executed exactly as you wrote it, as the whole of a `program.py`
with `__name__` set to `"__main__"`. It is a module body, so statements and
definitions sit side by side at the top level and there is no function to return
from. Write the `import` lines you would normally write.

A failed call raises `gg.core.ToolError`, so `except gg.core.ToolError as
failure` is how you handle one and `failure.code` is an enum member; let the rest
escape, and gg names the call that failed.

Required arguments are positional; optional ones are keyword arguments with
defaults. `import gg` is the line that reaches every module below, each an
ordinary Python module whose path is what you write.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "ruby")}}
Your response is executed exactly as you wrote it, as the whole of a
`program.rb`, at its top level. Statements and definitions sit side by side, and
the last expression's value goes nowhere. Write the `require` lines you would
normally write.

A failed call raises `GG::Core::ToolError`, which is a `StandardError` — so
`rescue GG::Core::ToolError => failure` is how you handle one, and
`failure.code` is a Symbol rather than free text; let the rest escape, and gg
names the call that failed.

Required arguments are positional, optional ones are keyword arguments with
defaults, `*items` takes as many as you like, and a `&body` is a block.
`require "gg"` is the line that reaches every module below, each a constant whose
path is what you write.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "rust")}}
Your response is compiled as the body of a function gg declares, so it is a
sequence of statements evaluated in order and there is no `main` to write. Every
item Rust allows in a function body is allowed where you wrote it, and is visible
to the whole program however far down you declared it.

Every call returns `Result<_, ToolError>`. A failure you do not handle ends the
turn with the failed call named, and matching on a `ToolError`'s `code` is how
you branch on the kind of failure. A failure of your own is built from a sentence
with `gg::program::message("no rows")`.

Required arguments are positional. A call with exactly one optional argument
takes an `Option<T>` in that position; a call with two or more takes an options
struct with a `Default`, filled in with functional-update syntax. Every module
below is in scope under the last segment of its path, because
`use gg::prelude::*;` is already written above your first line.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "swift")}}
Your response is compiled verbatim, as a whole Swift file. It is the top level of
a program, so bare statements run in the order you wrote them and every
declaration Swift has is legal beside them. The Swift standard library is there;
every other module is reached by writing its `import`.

A call that can fail is `throws` and its signature says so, so `try` is the whole
of the ceremony. A failure you expect is caught with
`catch let failure as core.ToolError`, whose `code` is an `enum`; let the rest
escape, and gg names the call that failed.

Required arguments are positional and optional ones have default values.
Everything whose role its name does not already carry has an argument label, so a
signature you look up shows you the labels the call is written with. `import gg`
is the line that brings every module below into scope, each a caseless `enum`
whose path is what you write — written out in full, with `gg` in front of it,
where a declaration of your own takes the short name.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq language.id "typescript")}}
Your response is evaluated as the body of a function whose parameters include
`gg`, `ToolError` and `lib`. Write statements at the top level; a `return` there
ends your program and its value goes nowhere.

A failed call throws, and a caught error is `unknown` until you narrow it:
`catch (e) { if (e instanceof ToolError) … }` is the shape that reads its `tool`
and `code`.

Required arguments are positional, and a call's optional arguments are the fields
of one options object in the last position. Every module below is bound under
`gg`, so a module's path is the call you write as it stands, and every type is
aliased bare as well, so a type name a signature writes is one you can copy.
{{/if}}

### Ending your session

Turns continue until you end your session.

A program that fails does not end your session: the ending it called is revoked.
Fix the fault and end your session in a program that runs to completion.

